WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:06.880
Ridley Scott's Alien is a sci-fi/horror classic,
a crazy cocktail of influences that somehow

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:10.360
manages to be more frightening than the sum
of its parts.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:14.370
Everybody who worked on it had their own idea
of what Alien would be about.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:19.510
Perhaps that's why scholars, sci-fi nerds,
and film critics alike continue to discover

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:23.350
creepy, unexplored channels within this terrifying
masterpiece.

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:26.710
So now let's take a look at the story behind
Alien.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:28.190
Dark Star

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:33.130
If you could trace the origin of Alien back
to one single point, it would have to be Dan

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:36.680
O'Bannon's 1974 sci-fi comedy Dark Star.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:42.370
While attending USC, O'Bannon made a seven-minute
horror-suspense short film called "Blood Bath."

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:47.310
The project caught the attention of a young
John Carpenter, also a student, and the two

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:51.980
set out to make a science fiction movie for
John Carpenter's master's thesis.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:56.270
They called their finished movie Dark Star,
and it...wasn't very good.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:01:01.060
It did have an alien in it...but, unfortunately,
that alien was a beach ball.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:01.560
With feet.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:13.400
According to O'Bannon, "Instead of the most
impressive student film ever made we had the

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.290
least impressive professional film ever made."

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:19.040
But that crappy film gave birth to a legend:

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.740
"Couple years down the line, I decided to
do Dark Star as a horror movie instead of

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.390
a comedy.

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:28.350
And that was the germ of Alien."

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:33.770
Not many people saw Dark Star, but future
Alien director Ridley Scott did — along

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:40.540
with visionary Chilean-French filmmaker Alejandro
Jodorowsky — and it left a lasting impression.

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:41.979
Dune Connections

00:01:41.979 --> 00:01:46.930
Jodorowsky was so impressed with Dark Star
that he hired O'Bannon to help him adapt Frank

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:50.070
Herbert's space opera Dune for the big screen.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:55.080
The project ultimately tanked, but Alien was
one of many successful projects that rose

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:58.570
from the ashes of Jodorowsky's ill-fated attempt.

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:03.270
O'Bannon moved to Paris to work on Dune, which
is where he met met artist Christopher Foss,

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:08.780
who'd later do design work for Alien, as well
as future Alien designer H.R. Giger.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:14.040
Jodorowsky's philosophy of art design also
heavily influenced O'Bannon's work on Alien.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:19.060
He would find his, quote, "spiritual warriors,"
and then put them together in one big room

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.550
to work out a movie's structure.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:26.190
Later, O'Bannon would use the same strategy
to brainstorm about Alien, bringing together

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:31.660
Foss, Giger, and cartoonists Moebius and Ron
Cobb to hash out the design for their little

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:34.070
sci-fi horror project.

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:35.070
Script struggles

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:40.790
Ultimately, Brandywine Productions agreed
to produce the film for 20th Century Fox,

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:42.990
with Ridley Scott in the director's chair.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:45.739
But the Nostromo still had some turbulence
ahead.

00:02:45.739 --> 00:02:51.120
According to O'Bannon, producers David Giler
and Walter Hill wanted to completely redo

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.209
his script.

00:02:52.209 --> 00:02:54.110
So that's exactly what they did.

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:58.890
According to Giler, he and Hill added "the
feminist elements everyone is talking about.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:01.550
We gave the characters texture, functions.

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:05.060
In O'Bannon's draft, they were totally different,
military types.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:06.060
All men.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:07.620
We changed all the dialogue.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:08.910
Every word of it."

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:13.030
O'Bannon's original screenplay featured the
xenomorph and the wrecked ship, and that's

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:15.500
about where the similarities to the finished
film end.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:20.860
Hill's and Giler's rewrites added Ripley and
Dallas and the rest of the gang, not to mention

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:26.690
the whole vast corporate conspiracy subplot
and the entire concept for human-looking androids,

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:30.280
an idea that was already an Alien cornerstone
by the first sequel.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:39.500
"Thank you."

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:46.360
But bizarrely, some drafts also had a time-travel-based
plotline featuring Jack the Ripper, Hercules,

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:51.819
and Genghis Khan, like a Magic Tree House
book...with murderous chest-bursting aliens.

00:03:51.819 --> 00:03:56.860
Then there's the Alien that, against all odds,
Scott and his team somehow made despite all

00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:57.920
the confusion.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:02.890
As artist Christopher Foss put it to Den of
Geek, "Poor old Dan O'Bannon, the bloke whose

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:05.670
concept it was, just got absolutely shafted…

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:10.380
I kind of got the impression that Ridley [Scott]
was quietly going his own way, trying to get

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:12.390
on with it and get it done."

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:13.760
Ship shape

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.799
The Nostromo, the dark, claustrophobic spaceship
where all the Alien action takes place, is

00:04:18.799 --> 00:04:21.339
practically another character in the movie.

00:04:21.339 --> 00:04:25.789
And Chris Foss and Ron Cobb were essential
to the creation of the vessel.

00:04:25.789 --> 00:04:30.320
But the final result didn't come right away
— the team worked on the design for five

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:35.120
whole months, pretty much the whole time the
producers were trying to find a director for

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:36.120
the movie.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:38.659
And some of those designs got...weird.

00:04:38.659 --> 00:04:45.020
Foss' had "endless spacecraft designs suggesting
submarines, diesel locomotives, Mayan interceptors,

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:48.169
Mississippi river boats, [and] jumbo space
arks."

00:04:48.169 --> 00:04:52.319
But in the end, they were simply too outlandish
for the producers to get on board.

00:04:52.319 --> 00:04:58.159
The Nostromo's final design came from one
of Ron Cobb's simpler sketches, and even then,

00:04:58.159 --> 00:05:02.339
Ridley Scott made the ship's modeler get rid
of Cobb's original yellow color scheme in

00:05:02.339 --> 00:05:05.580
favor of the flat gray that made it into the
film.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:06.979
Casting chaos

00:05:06.979 --> 00:05:11.919
Before Sigourney Weaver got the part of Ripley,
none other than Meryl Streep was on the short

00:05:11.919 --> 00:05:14.400
list of candidates to be offered the role.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.939
Streep is, of course, ridiculously talented.

00:05:16.939 --> 00:05:20.300
And Alien probably would have been just fine
with her in the lead role.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:21.699
But fate intervened.

00:05:21.699 --> 00:05:27.479
In 1978, Streep's romantic partner, actor
John Cazale, passed away, and nobody felt

00:05:27.479 --> 00:05:30.610
comfortable interrupting her mourning to make
the offer.

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:34.659
As it turns out, Ripley wasn't even Sigourney
Weaver's first choice.

00:05:34.659 --> 00:05:39.639
She said in an interview, "Actually the part
I wanted to play was Lambert, Veronica Cartwright's

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:40.639
part.

00:05:40.639 --> 00:05:44.979
In the first script I read, [Lambert] just
cracked jokes the whole time…That's the

00:05:44.979 --> 00:05:46.319
character I could identify with."

00:05:46.319 --> 00:05:51.309
And while Weaver wanted the part of Lambert,
Veronica Cartwright was dead set on playing

00:05:51.309 --> 00:05:52.309
Ripley.

00:05:52.309 --> 00:05:56.399
It was only just before shooting began that
Cartwright was told their roles had been switched.

00:05:56.399 --> 00:05:57.749
"I get a call.

00:05:57.749 --> 00:06:02.800
And they said, 'Okay we need you to come in
for your wardrobe for Lambert.'

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:05.200
And I said, h'old on I'm not Lambert, I'm
Ripley.'

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:06.560
'No, no, you're Lambert.'"

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.379
Obviously, the decisions all worked out in
the end, but it's interesting to consider

00:06:11.379 --> 00:06:15.349
how close we were to getting a completely
different movie.

00:06:15.349 --> 00:06:16.809
Silly scenes

00:06:16.809 --> 00:06:22.039
Ridley Scott worked hard to preserve the "less
is more" aesthetic that made Alien so terrifying,

00:06:22.039 --> 00:06:25.119
and that meant cutting a lot of scenes from
the final film.

00:06:25.119 --> 00:06:28.539
Some of the biggest cuts were made because
they looked...kinda stupid.

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:33.780
For example, Lambert's death is haunting because most of it is implied and offscreen.

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:39.400
The camera cuts away just before the alien gets her, so we don't actually see what happens.

00:06:39.409 --> 00:06:41.029
But her death was filmed.

00:06:41.029 --> 00:06:42.679
It just looked way too cheesy.

00:06:42.679 --> 00:06:48.349
By the same token, Brett's death in the final
film is terrifying, but the extended cut of

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:50.940
that scene is borderline absurd:

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:56.900
"I'm not gonna hurt ya."

00:06:57.880 --> 00:06:58.939
Academic legacy

00:06:58.939 --> 00:07:04.330
For a sci-fi horror creature feature, Alien
gets a surprising amount of love in the academic

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:05.330
world.

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:11.199
Case in point: June 2017 saw the publication
of a book called Alien and Philosophy, a collection

00:07:11.199 --> 00:07:13.469
of essays on all things Alien.

00:07:13.469 --> 00:07:18.110
It's heady stuff, but it just goes to show
there's more to the xenomorph than a facehugger,

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:21.409
a chestburster, and "a man in a crocodile
suit."

00:07:21.409 --> 00:07:26.150
If the grotesque imagery, state-of-the-art
special effects, and storied production aren't

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:30.560
enough to build a compelling Film Theory thesis,
then maybe all those Freudian themes will

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:31.689
do the trick.

00:07:31.689 --> 00:07:32.740
Thanks for watching!

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:35.809
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:35.809 --> 00:07:38.369
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

